    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p4-q8-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "If $\\log_2 a=12$ and $\\log_2 b=3$, then find $\\log_2\\frac{a}{b}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$9$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}a&=2^{12}\\\\b&=2^3\\\\\\frac{a}{b}&=\\frac{2^{12}}{2^3}\\\\\\frac{a}{b}&=2^9\\\\\\log_2\\frac{a}{b}&=9\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p4-q8-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "If $\\log_5 a=9$ and $\\log_5 b=3$, then find $\\log_5\\frac{a}{b}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$6$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}a&=5^9\\\\b&=5^3\\\\\\frac{a}{b}&=\\frac{5^9}{5^3}\\\\\\frac{a}{b}&=5^6\\\\\\log_5\\frac{a}{b}&=6\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p4-q8-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "What do you notice about the previous two questions? Can you make and prove a conjecture?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p4-q8-c-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\log_k\\frac{a}{b}=\\log_k a-\\log_k b",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", for a valid base and positive a and b.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p4-q8-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Write a and b as powers of the base, divide using the index law, then convert back to logarithmic form.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "In both cases, the logarithm of the quotient equals the difference of the logarithms.\n\nConjecture: for $k>0$, $k\\ne1$ and $a,b>0$,\n$$\\log_k\\frac{a}{b}=\\log_k a-\\log_k b.$$\nLet $m=\\log_k a$ and $n=\\log_k b$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}a&=k^m\\\\b&=k^n\\\\\\frac{a}{b}&=\\frac{k^m}{k^n}\\\\\\frac{a}{b}&=k^{m-n}\\\\\\log_k\\frac{a}{b}&=m-n\\\\\\log_k\\frac{a}{b}&=\\log_k a-\\log_k b\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 54
      }
    ]
  },
